{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Abraham made a great feast.</b> There is no doubt that the great ones of the land were at that feast, for the wonder surrounding Isaac was great, and the wealth in Abraham's house was abundant; and it is possible that there were kings and counselors of the land there, for we have already found that the kings of the land sought his welfare and wished to make a covenant with him. But because it was an occasion of feasting and rejoicing, their names were not specified. Now we find in the house of mourning that Scripture specifies the names of those who came there — the companions of Job, of whom it is written (Job 2:11): ״ויבאו איש ממקומו אליפז התימני ובלדד השוחי וצופר הנעמתי״. And from this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 7:2): ״טוב ללכת אל בית אבל מלכת אל בית משתה״. Scripture has clarified to us that all the rejoicing of this world is vanity and emptiness." "<b>On the day Isaac was weaned.</b> It is the way of the world that a person makes a feast on the day a son is born to him or on the day of circumcision, in honor of the commandment. And the reason Abraham delayed making a feast for Isaac until the day he weaned him may be explained thus: from the day he was weaned, he set him apart for Torah study, and one should not wonder at this, for at three years of age Abraham recognized his Creator. Therefore he did not wish to make the feast neither on the day of birth nor on the day of circumcision, but left the matter until the day he weaned him, so that he might rejoice in his son with the joy of Torah, of which it is written (Psalms 19:9): ״פקודי ה' ישרים משמחי לב״. And as it is written (Isaiah 28:9): ״את מי יורה דעה ואת מי יבין שמועה גמולי מחלב״. For as soon as he was weaned, he was consecrated to be set apart and dedicated to the service of HaShem and prepared to be a perfect burnt offering." "<b>And know</b> further that a father's love for his son is not on the day of birth nor on the day of circumcision, but on the day he is weaned; and then his love grows stronger and stronger." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'On the day Isaac was weaned' — on the day HaShem circumcised Isaac (ביום ה״ג מל את יצחק). And from here there is support for our custom that we make a feast on the day of circumcision, for according to this Midrash Abraham made a great feast on the eighth day of Isaac's circumcision; and it is a fitting custom, and concerning it David said (Psalms 50:5): ״אספו לי חסידי כורתי בריתי עלי זבח״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {3 "The Midrash reads ה״ג as an abbreviation for ה׳ גמול or similar, interpreting ״הגמל״ (weaning) as ״ה׳ גמל״ (HaShem circumcised); the abbreviation is left untranslated as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:00.709286436Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}